WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.160
﻿I’m a music teacher &amp; professional musician&nbsp;
who has made more than 500 keyboard &amp; digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.880
piano reviews &amp; comparisons on my channel and&nbsp;
I’m going to help you pick a keyboard, digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:29.960
piano or MIDI controller that best suits your&nbsp;
budget and the type of music you want to create.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:58.200
Before I begin, here’s a quick tip. If you&nbsp;
are an absolute beginner, don’t spend too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:04.000
on your very first keyboard. You don’t need an&nbsp;
expensive keyboard to learn the basics. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.000
if you can stretch your budget to buy a mid-tier&nbsp;
piano keyboard, you will find that they sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:16.000
much better, have better key action, are more&nbsp;
solidly built and have a higher resale value.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.160
If you are mostly interested in learning and&nbsp;
playing piano pieces, get a digital piano on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:27.920
list that has 88 weighted keys. If you are more&nbsp;
into contemporary pop, rock, jazz &amp; world music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.280
or playing with a band, a synth action keyboard&nbsp;
is lightweight, cheaper and better for playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:39.880
non-piano sounds. A MIDI controller keyboard&nbsp;
does not come with any onboard sounds. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:45.120
you use a MIDI keyboard to control virtual&nbsp;
instruments and music production software that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:51.760
is installed on your computer, iPad or smartphone.&nbsp;
This video is not sponsored and I paid for these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.720
keyboards. The prices I mention are accurate&nbsp;
as at the time of this video and in order not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.080
to make this video 2 hours long, my in-depth&nbsp;
reviews, sound demonstration and best prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:09.200
for the keyboards and digital pianos in this list&nbsp;
are in the video description and comment section.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.520
$100+
The beginner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:18.400
keyboard with the biggest bang for your buck&nbsp;
is the $199 Yamaha’s PSR-E383. It comes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:25.480
61 touch sensitive synth action keys, 650 sounds&nbsp;
- which can be layered and split, more than 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:33.960
DSP effects and 260 rhythm accompaniments using&nbsp;
Yamaha’s time tested AWM sampling technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:38.920
There are music lessons built into the keyboard&nbsp;
and you can use the onboard speakers or plug in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:44.040
pair of headphones. To record your performances,&nbsp;
there’s a 2 track song recorder and it can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:50.040
powered with an electrical outlet or using&nbsp;
6 “AA” batteries. The USB port supports both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:56.720
MIDI &amp; digital audio for use with music learning&nbsp;
apps and music production software. For $100 more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:02.920
there’s the 76-keys version PSR-EW320&nbsp;
- great for playing piano pieces or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:09.000
key-lights version EZ310 - which lights up&nbsp;
the keys to show you which notes to press.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.560
If producing contemporary digital music&nbsp;
using virtual instruments and multi-track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:20.880
recording software is more your cup of tea,&nbsp;
you won’t go wrong with the $100+ Impact GX61&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:27.000
MIDI controller keyboard from Nektar. It&nbsp;
has 61 touch sensitive synth action keys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:33.240
DAW transport controls, advanced MIDI integration&nbsp;
right out of the box for 10 of the most popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:38.600
music production software including the&nbsp;
bundled 8-track sequencer from Bitwig.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:43.080
My full review, sound demo and best&nbsp;
prices for these $100+ keyboards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.960
can be found in the description &amp; comment section.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:52.600
$200+
At $219, the Casio CT-S1 is a fantastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:58.880
61 keys keyboard for those who want one of the&nbsp;
best key actions and studio quality sounds at an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:06.160
affordable price in an ultra-portable form factor&nbsp;
without all the bells &amp; whistles. You get 61 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:13.120
the most usable studio quality tones powered by&nbsp;
Casio’s latest AiX sound chip. These sounds can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:20.640
layered and there are more than a few dozen reverb&nbsp;
&amp; DSP effects. A power adaptor or 6 “AA” batteries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:27.080
power the onboard speakers and you can plug in a&nbsp;
pair of headphones. The micro-USB port supports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:33.960
MIDI for music learning apps and with a Bluetooth&nbsp;
adaptor you get wireless MIDI and audio streaming.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:40.560
For an additional $30 more than the CT-S1,&nbsp;
the Casio CT-S400 in a similar ultra-portable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.200
design gets you 600 tones which can&nbsp;
be layered and split and 200 rhythm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:51.120
accompaniments for you to jam along with. You&nbsp;
get a good variety of reverb and DSP effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.960
a 6 track song recorder and 32 user memory&nbsp;
to store your settings. The keyboard can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.680
powered with 6 “AA” batteries or with&nbsp;
a power adaptor and you can use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:07.440
onboard speakers or a pair of headphones.&nbsp;
A micro-USB MIDI port lets you connect to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:13.080
music learning apps and a Bluetooth adaptor&nbsp;
gets you wireless MIDI and audio streaming.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:22.320
For $299, there’s the Yamaha EZ310 and PSR-EW320.&nbsp;
The EZ310 is the key-lights version of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:29.760
PSR-E383 I talked about earlier on my list.&nbsp;
It is pretty much identical to the PSR-E383&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:36.040
except for the colour - white instead of black&nbsp;
and you get an additional 27 preset songs for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:43.080
use with the key light and lesson features.&nbsp;
The Yamaha PSR-EW320 is also identical to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:50.880
the PSR-E383 but with 76 instead of 61 keys&nbsp;
with everything else remaining the same. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:57.280
EW-320 is a good choice if you want the&nbsp;
extra 15 keys for your piano repertoire.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:03.720
For digital music producers, the $269&nbsp;
Arturia Keylab Essential 61 MK3 is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:09.720
excellent choice. The 61 keys MIDI controller&nbsp;
comes with a great velocity sensitive hybrid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:15.600
synth-piano key action, a rotary knob, 9&nbsp;
encoders, 9 faders, transport switches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:19.840
modulation and pitch bend wheels and&nbsp;
8 pressure sensitive pads for finger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:26.360
drumming or triggering scenes. More than 2,000&nbsp;
sounds and virtual instruments from Arturia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:31.840
Native Instruments &amp; UVI that cost up to&nbsp;
$1,000 are bundled with the controller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:37.560
keyboard together with the industry leading&nbsp;
music production software Ableton Live Lite.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.560
My in-depth review, sound demo&nbsp;
and best prices for these $200+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:46.040
keyboards can be found in the&nbsp;
description &amp; comment section.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:52.640
$300+
For $349, Roland’s latest GO:Keys 3 keyboard is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:07:00.640
unbeatable. Packed with more than 1,154 instrument&nbsp;
sounds - powered by Roland’s flagship Zen-Core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:07.120
sound engine - which can be layered and split&nbsp;
across the keyboard, 203 performance ready rhythm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:15.280
accompaniments, a user definable chord sequencer,&nbsp;
more than 100 effects, 256 user programmable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:21.680
settings, onboard speakers and user expandable&nbsp;
sound &amp; rhythms packs using Roland SoundCloud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:27.720
you will not find a more value for money&nbsp;
&amp; competent 61-key keyboard at this price.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:34.200
With both USB &amp; Bluetooth, you get both wired &amp;&nbsp;
wireless MIDI &amp; audio streaming for use with music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:40.680
learning apps &amp; music production software. The&nbsp;
onboard song recorder records both MIDI &amp; digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:45.960
audio which is great for sharing online and&nbsp;
this keyboard can be powered with batteries or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:52.880
a power adaptor. The GO:Keys 3 is available&nbsp;
in 3 beautiful colours to fit your style.

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:59.800
Also at $349 is the Korg Liano, the best&nbsp;
sounding ultra lightweight 88-key digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:05.760
piano at this price. With 8 studio quality&nbsp;
Italian &amp; German piano &amp; electric piano&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:10.880
samples and loads of bundled virtual&nbsp;
instruments, this is a keyboard built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:16.720
specifically for piano players. You get&nbsp;
reverb &amp; chorus effects, onboard speakers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:22.800
a headphones jack, a sustain pedal input and&nbsp;
a USB port that handles both MIDI &amp; digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:28.640
audio - great for music learning apps and music&nbsp;
production software. The keyboard can be powered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:34.760
with 6 “AA” batteries or a power adaptor&nbsp;
and it’s available in 6 striking colours.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:40.520
If you need an affordable 88-weighted keys&nbsp;
digital piano and do not need portability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:48.520
the Donner DEP-20 at just $350 is a great&nbsp;
bargain. With 238 instrument sounds which can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:54.600
be layered and split across the keyboard, 200&nbsp;
rhythm accompaniments, onboard song recorder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:59.720
powerful speakers, a headphones jack and&nbsp;
USB MIDI for connecting to music apps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:05.120
the Donner DEP-20 gives tremendous&nbsp;
value for an absolute beginner.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:13.000
If you pay $29 more, get the Alesis Recital&nbsp;
Pro. At $379, you get 88 touch sensitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:18.880
weighted piano style keys and 12 piano,&nbsp;
electric piano and organ sounds which can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:24.200
be layered or split across the keyboard. A&nbsp;
simple song recorder, sustain pedal jack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:29.960
reverb &amp; chorus effects, headphones jack and&nbsp;
USB MIDI for use with music learning apps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:35.200
and music production software is included.&nbsp;
The onboard speakers can be powered with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:41.520
power adaptor or with 6 “D” size batteries.&nbsp;
You may find cheaper China brand 88 weighted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:47.120
keys digital pianos but do yourself a favour&nbsp;
and don’t be penny wise and pound foolish.

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:54.400
For the digital music producer who is more focused&nbsp;
on piano repertoire, the $399 Arturia Keylab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.400 --> 00:10:03.200
Essential 88 MK3 is what you’re looking for. It&nbsp;
is identical to the $269 Keylab Essential 61 MK3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:11.280
I talked about earlier, but with more keys and is&nbsp;
an absolute bargain for those who require 88-keys.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:14.320
Check the description &amp; comment&nbsp;
section for my in-depth review,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:20.920
sound demo and best prices&nbsp;
for these $300+ keyboards.

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:23.720
$400+
2 of the best keyboards in the $400+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:35.640
category are the $499 Roland GO:Keys 5 &amp; $489&nbsp;
Yamaha PSR-EW425. The Yamaha has 76 keys whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:45.560
the Roland has 61 keys but the Roland has 1,154&nbsp;
sounds versus the 820 sounds on the Yamaha. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:53.920
Yamaha has more accompaniment styles - 290 versus&nbsp;
the 203 rhythms on the Roland but the GO:Keys 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:59.440
gives you 4 variations for each style and it’s&nbsp;
really easy to expand the sounds and rhythm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:07.120
accompaniments on the Roland using CloudConnect.&nbsp;
The Yamaha PSR-EW425 has more powerful onboard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:13.880
speakers and has 2 live control knobs which the&nbsp;
Roland does not have. While both can record songs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:20.520
on MIDI and digital audio, only the Roland has&nbsp;
the option for Bluetooth wireless connectivity.

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:26.080
The Alesis Recital Grand, also sold as&nbsp;
the Alesis Prestige in some markets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:34.000
is the only 88 graded hammer action digital pianos&nbsp;
in the $400+ price category I can recommend. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:39.080
get 16 high quality multi-sampled piano&nbsp;
and electric piano sounds which can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:44.080
layered and split across the keyboard,&nbsp;
a powerful 50 watt array speaker system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:50.760
dual headphone jacks, triple pedal support and a&nbsp;
USB MIDI port for use with music learning apps.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:54.760
You can find my demonstration of the&nbsp;
sounds and my in-depth review of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:59.240
$400+ keyboards and digital pianos&nbsp;
in the description &amp; comment section.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:03.120
$500+
$599 seems to be the sweet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:10.040
spot for a solid beginner 88 graded hammer action&nbsp;
digital piano. You won’t go wrong with either the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:19.760
Roland FP-10, Yamaha P-145 or Casio PX-S1100.&nbsp;
The Roland FP-10 has 36 instrument sounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:24.880
the most number in this category and the&nbsp;
Yamaha has the least at just 10 sounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:32.040
but the Casio has the highest polyphony&nbsp;
at 192 notes. The Casio PX-S1100 is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:38.200
the most compact, lightweight and can be battery&nbsp;
operated whereas the other 2 can only be powered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:45.200
off an electrical outlet. I prefer the key action&nbsp;
of the Roland FP-10 but the Casio has a lighter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:52.200
action which beginners may benefit from. They all&nbsp;
have headphones jack and onboard speakers but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.200 --> 00:13:00.360
amplification on the Casio is the most powerful&nbsp;
of the 3. Both the Roland FP-10 and Casio PX-S1100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:05.360
have both USB MIDI and wireless Bluetooth&nbsp;
functionality to connect to music learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:12.160
apps and audio streaming while the Yamaha&nbsp;
P-145 has just a USB MIDI port. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:18.840
you will not go wrong with any of these 3 if you&nbsp;
are looking for your 1st beginner digital piano.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:25.940
My in-depth review, sound demo and best prices&nbsp;
are in the description and comment section.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:29.680
$600+
At $699,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:37.920
the Roland FP-30X and Yamaha P-255 are great&nbsp;
choices for serious piano students who are on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:44.160
tight budget. You get really good key action&nbsp;
at these prices from both manufacturers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:50.480
there is a perceptible jump in sound quality,&nbsp;
build quality and note polyphony which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:59.040
important. The Yamaha P-255 has 24 high quality&nbsp;
tones whereas Roland packed more than 321 tones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:07.080
in the FP-30X. You get dual headphone jacks,&nbsp;
triple pedal support, a song recorder, and stereo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:13.480
aux audio outputs for connecting to more powerful&nbsp;
speakers or mixing decks for performances. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:18.840
both keyboards have USB MIDI for use with music&nbsp;
learning apps and music production software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:26.240
the Roland FP-30X supports wireless Bluetooth.&nbsp;
Do note that the speakers on the Yamaha P-255&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:33.680
are significantly more powerful than those found&nbsp;
on the FP-30X if that’s important to you. These 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:40.340
digital pianos are entry-level pro keyboards that&nbsp;
are often found in clubs, pubs and restaurants.

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:43.240
$800+
The $800+ mark is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:52.560
you will find my personal favourites - the hybrid&nbsp;
arranger digital pianos. The $849 Yamaha DGX-670&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.560 --> 00:15:02.000
and the $899 Roland FP-E50 hybrid piano arrangers&nbsp;
are the best of both worlds. They have 88 graded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:08.880
hammer action keys and have rhythm accompaniments&nbsp;
and music production capabilities. The Roland has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:15.280
more than 1,000 sounds whereas the Yamaha has&nbsp;
600 voices but the Yamaha makes up for it by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:24.080
having 263 rhythm accompaniment styles whereas&nbsp;
the Roland has just 177 styles. However, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:30.600
expand the sounds and rhythm accompaniments on the&nbsp;
Roland FP-E50 using expansion packs available on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:37.400
Roland’s CloudConnect whereas the DGX-670 has&nbsp;
more limited expansion options. The Yamaha has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:43.520
4 variations for each rhythm accompaniment as&nbsp;
well as more granular rhythm track controls. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:49.720
rhythm style creator is also available on the&nbsp;
DGX-670 whereas the Roland does not have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:56.720
feature but has a chord sequencer instead.&nbsp;
Both hybrid arranger pianos have a mic input&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:03.160
and vocal effects if you want to sing and play.&nbsp;
However, I do wish the Roland has a colour high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:10.640
definition LCD screen like the Yamaha but it only&nbsp;
has a monochrome screen. The Roland FP-E50 does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:18.160
make up for it by having speakers that are twice&nbsp;
as powerful as the Yamaha DGX-670. You get every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:24.880
imaginable connectivity option including wireless&nbsp;
Bluetooth but the Yamaha has a powerful 16 track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:31.160
sequencer whereas the song recorder on the&nbsp;
Roland is a basic one. The bottom line is this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:37.840
if you want loads of sounds and expansion, get&nbsp;
the Roland FP-E50. If rhythm accompaniments and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:43.840
music arranging is more important to you,&nbsp;
the Yamaha DGX-670 is the better choice.

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:45.960
$1,000
Here are my recommendations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:57.440
in the $1,000 category. For digital pianos, the&nbsp;
$949 Kawai ES-120 and $999 Roland RD-08 are my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:03.240
favourites. They feature great sound quality,&nbsp;
fantastic graded hammer action keys and solid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:08.520
build quality. If you want a pro-level arranger&nbsp;
keyboard with rhythm accompaniments and more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:18.040
1,200 sounds, the $1,099 Yamaha PSR-SX600 is the&nbsp;
one you want to get. For gigs and performances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:25.320
the lightweight 61-key Yamaha CK61 stage&nbsp;
keyboard and the Roland Juno DS88 hammer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.320 --> 00:17:32.200
action stage synthesiser with thousands of sounds&nbsp;
and every imaginable feature a working keyboardist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:40.320
needs gets my recommendation. The CK61 and Juno&nbsp;
DS88 can be powered with batteries or a power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:48.694
adapter and these keyboards also work really&nbsp;
well as an advanced MIDI keyboard controller.

00:17:48.694 --> 00:17:50.680
$1,000 Home Digital Pianos
For the more traditional console&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.680 --> 00:17:56.240
home digital pianos that costs about $1,000,&nbsp;
the ones that impresses me the most when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.240 --> 00:18:04.880
comes to sound quality and key action are the&nbsp;
made in Japan Korg LP-380U, the Yamaha YDP-S35,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:12.240
the Casio PX-870 and if you can stretch your&nbsp;
budget a little more, the Roland F701 when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:18.640
it comes to bang for buck. Home console pianos&nbsp;
usually cost more due to the cabinet work, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:24.960
significantly larger speakers and amplification&nbsp;
system, a bench and a triple pedal system.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:30.600
You can find everything I recommend including&nbsp;
the music learning apps my students use in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:35.120
description and comment section. I hope you&nbsp;
found this video useful. My name is Jeremy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:40.640
See and check out my in-depth video reviews&nbsp;
and sound demo if you need more information.

